A||||7||jlruiz67||3.4 and 3.4L DOHC question||||1||
Z||000000||jlruiz67||07-13-2000||08:38 PM||||Hello all<BR>I'm in the planning stages of a 3.4L conversion and like to know if anyone has done this. I'm debating betwen 3.4L and 3.4L DOHC. Is the DOHC much harder to do? can I use the stock Exhaust System on any of them? I like to keep the stock exhaust untill I can afford an upgrade. I have to do this in the low cost side (cheap) because I don't have the money tree growing in the yard anymore and I actually have to work for my money  hehehe. The work I will be doing mostly myself with the limited experience that I have. I can read and follow directions and have lots of patience. I also like to know if anyone has done the 3.4L DOHC, how hard is it? Can anyone compare the 2 diferent motors in performance and cost to do. I have a 87 GT Automatic and it has 200,000 miles on it and that is why I want the change. I don't want to spend the money on a 2.8 rebuild if I can drop a 3.4L or 3.4L DOHC for about the same price. Thanks for your help.||||reg||1
Z||000001||lowCG||07-13-2000||08:51 PM||||The DOHC swap is better for someone who doesn't want to mess around with tuning and adjustments,the work is a matter of following an electrical schematic,asking a few questions here and there,a lot of soldering,and some grinding with a 4" Makita.<BR>An exhaust can be made with two 4" round VW cats across the back of the subframe,about $120 after welding.<BR>For $1500 I got a 30K motor,another $500 for mounts,clutch,$100 for hoses/clamps,and then the exhaust.<BR>The stock Fiero motor is pretty nice the way it is too.||||reg||1
Z||000002||jlruiz67||07-13-2000||09:05 PM||||Is it really that simple? I also heard that the timing belt insted of chain in the DOHC is a problem and has to be replaced every 30,000 miles. Is the DOHC a better engine than the 3.4L? Is it really that simple???<P>Thank you much for your past response <P><BR>||||reg||14
Z||000003||olympic||07-13-2000||10:08 PM||||  The Camaro 3.4 is the easiest.  You just redrill the starter holes on the opposite side of the block, bolt on the Fiero heads and intake and install the engine in the Fiero.  The 3.4 DOHC will require lots of rewiring and plumbing.  The actual mounting of the engine is pretty straight forward. With your automatic trans, the Camaro engine may be the way to go.  The DOHC doesn't start to make good power until 3000 rpm where it really screams up to 7000+ rpm.  The Camaro engine will have lots of low down torque to get you moving.  Horsepower ratings would be: ~160hp fro the Camaro 3.4 and 215(at nearly 7000 rpm though) for the DOHC.<P>And yes, the DOHC is hard on timing chains and tensioners.  I think the 30,000 mile replacement estimate is low though.<P>------------------<BR>"Born free....taxed to death."||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||07-13-2000||10:44 PM||||The DOHC cam belt is easy to replace. Just bring it to TDC, clamp the cam gears, and pop on the belt. It's not that bad.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex4mula||07-14-2000||08:29 AM||||I have seen many persons here talking about the 3.4DOHC swap in a way that seems easy. It is not. I did the Camaro 3.4 myself and that one is straightfoward. Just remove and replace back. All is stock. The hardest item in that one is drilling the starter holes. I paid Ed Parks of that. My friend is doing a DOHC 3.4. It is NOT straightfoward. Mounts needs to be modified, computer wiring required and new exaust. If you want something ok for a normal home mechanic with common tools do the Camaro 3.4.||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||07-14-2000||10:05 AM||||Both are straightforward,yet the 2valve 3.4 can be done quickly,where the DOHC takes more time and planning.You also must be able to read and follow a schematic,this is important.||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||07-14-2000||11:19 AM||||I just meant that the cam belt is easy to replace. The engine swap is more work. Any swap is. The DOHC has a few gotcha's but it's still not that bad. 100 hours or so.<P>Terry||||reg||1
